it can be tempting at times of darkness  to seek advice from earlier generations  of world leaders  gracia michelle is a veteran advocate  for global peace women's rights and  humanitarian justice she was also at  various times wife of nelson mandela and  wife of samora michelle the president of  mozambique now as a deputy chair of the  elders she works alongside figures such  as jimmy carter mary robinson and ban  ki-moon  first up thank you so much uh for taking  some time to talk to us at sky news  today i just wanted to reflect with you  a bit on where we are in the world  there was a time i think when everything  felt positive at the turn of the century  your late husband nelson mandela was  free dictatorships were in retreat the  un millennium goals were about tackling  poverty and now i look around the world  and we have war in ukraine ethiopia  yemen and elsewhere humanitarian crises  authoritarian regimes global warning is  accelerating  from your point of view grass where did  it all go so wrong we have to recognize  that  most of the  crises  we are faced with today  are man-made  so  i believe it's mostly  a failure of leadership  if i want to give an example  the  climate change issue  oh  how many years we have been told  year after year  with unequivocal  statistics showing exactly how the  situation is getting out of control but  the leaders of the world they don't seem  to be listening  they don't seem to be taking the with  the same magnitude of the crisis  measures which would be  tackling and preventing the situation  where we are today going back to africa  there was optimism  about africa about development but  there's still conflict there's still  positive poverty  massive unfilled human potential why is  it taking so long again does it for you  go back to leadership not just  leadership within african nations but  also global leadership from other  leaders around the world is it back to  that again  definitely  because  why for instance a country like ethiopia  would be involved with itself  there's no other answer it's because  leaders are failing to one  to develop governments which will  involve everyone in the specific  countries to be in charge  second  they fail i mean to be inclusive  bringing  regions bringing different ages bringing  different skills to be part of the  decision making in their own countries  and of course  terrorism will use all the gaps which  exist i mean to flourish so it is not  drought it is no doubt an issue of  leadership  but you are asking about the world as  well and the climate change is a good  example of  failure  globally  how many times since copenhagen  the leaders of the world they pledged  to  give a hundred billion dollars every  year to tackle climate change but  particularly for mitigation adaptation  and supporting poor countries i mean who  did not make the climate change i mean  reach the levels in which we are and  they promise and promise every year and  nothing happens so you find failure of  leadership  within  africa yes but on climate change it's  mostly global because africa is paying  the price of  a situation it did not create  and  i believe we need we need to encourage  our young people and women i will insist  on women as well because they are most  imp impacted by this i mean to  take leadership to voice  what are the aspirations and to mobilize  so that we have  different kind of leadership which is  going to take into account the feelings  the aspirations the visions which are  coming from people themselves  and just finally there's been some  coverage recently about the commonwealth  uh with prince william  saying um that he won't necessarily head  the commonwealth he might not be head of  the commonwealth do you think an  organization like the commonwealth still  has any resonance uh with africans and  african nations  i think uh  any  kind of organization  which build the bridges  amongst peoples of the world amongst the  nations it's welcome  what i believe the  commonwealth should  do is  to perhaps to  revisit  the reasons why these nations  across the globe are coming together  not to be based on the past yes  but it to be based on  interest  of these nations  as part of the human family today and  tomorrow so  yes it is relevant but i think it needs  to reinvent itself to readjust to what  are the issues which would bring these  nations together congress i love the  idea of  building bridges between nations and  that the commonwealth can stand for that  like it can be nations with common  interests working together on issues  such as climate change but i guess the  question is do you think that the royal  family  of the united kingdom should head that  group of nations  well i will leave  i will live with the nations who are  part of the commonwealth to to to decide  that because i don't think it's a  personal opinion is my but  you have you seen what has happened  recently in the caribbean  do you follow what is it  what is the message which is coming from  thee and i think it's not against the  royal family it is mostly readjustment  of the kind of relationship in which  it's a relationship amongst the equals  within an organization  and  that is what i'm trying to say in my own  words of how  an organization can adjust itself  the the the the the uk  is is a country which  yes in the past had  a certain kind of a role in the world  but the uk today  has to recognize that democratic  institutions are the ones which are  prevailing and  how  does this happen i'm just suggesting  listen to what the caribbeans are saying  okay grassa it was an absolute pleasure  uh to talk to you thank you so much  and i love those roses on your jacket  they look absolutely gorgeous um thank  you so much for your time it was an  absolute honour to talk to you thank you  the wonderful grass and michelle there  well thank you to all our guests this  evening don't forget to download the  beth rigby interviews podcast for more  content
 

